.TH WRITE 2
.SH NAME
write \- write on a file
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B #include <unistd.h>
.PP
.B int write(int fildes, const char *buffer, int nbytes);
.SH DESCRIPTION
A file descriptor is a word returned from a
successful
.I open,
.I creat,
.I dup,
or
.IR pipe (2)
call.
.PP
.I Buffer
is the address of
.I nbytes
contiguous
bytes which are written on the output file.
The number of characters actually written is returned.
It should be regarded as an error
if this is not the same as requested.
.PP
Writes which are multiples
of a block long and begin on a block boundary
in the file
are more efficient than any others.
.B Note:
v7 said 512 characters.
.B A block is BSIZE == 3072 bytes here,
which is 512
.I words.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
creat(2), open(2), pipe(2)
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
Returns
\-1 on error: bad descriptor, buffer address, or
count; physical I/O errors.
.SH ASSEMBLER
.B Note:
v7's page gave the PDP-11 form here \- a decimal call number and a
`\fBsys\fP \fIname\fP; \fIarg\fP ...' line.  Neither is how a call is made on this
machine.  The gate is one extracode \-
.B $77 SYS_write
\- and the C calling convention has already put the arguments where it reads them:
1..n\-1 below r15, the last in the accumulator, the result in the accumulator, the
error number in r14.
.PP
.B The numbering itself is v7's, unchanged.
The macro is defined in <sys/syscall.h>, which is the only place a syscall number
appears on the user side.  See
.IR intro (2)
for the whole contract.
